The observing strategy required to discover or confirm super- and sub-Alfvénic M-SPI depends on several characteristics of the radio emission . Namely, the radio emission is:
- Beamed along the surface of a cone with a large opening angle ( ≳ 70 ◦ );
- Limited in frequency by the magnetic field strength of the emitting body ( 𝜈 ∝ 𝐵 );
- Often highly circularly polarised ( > 60%); and,
- Modulated by either the orbital motion of the exoplanet or the rotation of the host body (Hess and Zarka, 2011; Kavanagh and Vedantham, 2023; Louis et al., 2025). Usually this modulation results in a low ( ≲ 10%) duty cycle of emission (see Fig. 5) but provides peculiar arch-like structures in the dynamic spectrum.
Since the radio emission is beamed, has a low duty cycle or is inherently stochastic in brightness, there is difficulty in a priori identifying radio bright M-SPI systems despite stellar system parameters having ideal parameters for emission (Louis et al., 2025). Moreover circularly polarised ECMI emission can be generated by stars due to their own magnetic activity, unrelated to any M-SPI. For example, despite almost all M dwarfs host a short period planet (Dressing and Charbonneau, 2015), Callingham et al. (2021) only identified ≈ 0.5% of all M dwarf systems within 50 pc as being radio-bright at 144 MHz at ∼ mJy sensitivity. Therefore, even in the era of the SKA telescopes, wide-field circularly polarised surveys will remain the workhorse for discovering M-SPI candidates.

