Using these criteria, we identify five known binary MSPs that are particularly promising candidates for microhertz GW searches with the SKAO, which are listed in Table 1. Of these five, two (J1903 + 0327 and J2234 + 0611) are included in the NANOGrav PTA (Agazie et al., 2023b), and another (J0955 -6150) is included in the MeerKAT PTA (Miles et al., 2024a). The five systems all have significant orbital eccentricities ( 𝑒 ≳ 0 . 1), and cover a range of orbital periods from ∼ 7.4 to ∼ 95 days (orbital frequencies from ∼ 1.6 to ∼ 0.12 µ Hz), allowing them to collectively span the full breadth of the microhertz band once higher harmonics are included.

1. The old AGN plasma can be transported away from its original position by the motions of the intragroup medium driven by the large-scale dynamics of the system, such as sloshing motions (Kolokythas et al., 2020; Brienza et al., 2022; Shulevski et al., 2024);
2. The old AGN plasma can extend over large portions of a galaxy group, reaching distances up to R500 (e.g., Brienza et al., 2021; Rajpurohit et al., 2025). This implies that potentially the volume of galaxy groups may be filled with a non-thermal plasma component, as shown in Fig. 4;
3. The old AGN bubbles can fragment into thin (a few kpc) and long (up to hundreds of kpc) filamentary structures, possibly in the presence of a turbulent environment. These filaments are likely supported by magnetic fields, which can act against instabilities and slow down the complete mixing of the non-thermal plasma with the external medium and the full thermalisation of its energy. Indeed, as the resolution and sensitivity of modern instruments improve, it is becoming clear that filamentary structures are often present in non-thermal emission, from radio galaxy lobes, to radio relics, to isolated filaments in the ICM/IGrM (e.g., Owen et al., 2014; Brienza et al., 2021; Rudnick et al., 2022; Churazov et al., 2025). This systems offer therefore key opportunities to study magnetic fields and cosmic-ray evolution, particularly in galaxy groups where they seem to be relatively common.

The frequency of these occurrences, and how it scales with the mass and dynamical state of the system, remains, however, to be investigated. Particularly interesting are systems where multiple bubbles associated with the BGG are detected simultaneously, as this allows direct estimates of the jet duty cycle through spectral ageing modelling. To date, this type of analysis has been performed for only a handful of sources (e.g., Candini et al., 2023; Brienza et al., 2022, 2025), as it requires long observing campaigns with multiple instruments to cover a sufficient portion of the radio spectrum at matched sensitivity and uv -coverage. Results from these case studies suggest jet active phases lasting a few tens of Myr, interleaved with quiescent periods ranging from a few Myr up to ∼ 100 Myr. Expanding the systematic study of jet duty cycles in galaxy groups in the SKA era will be crucial for quantifying the cumulative energy released by jets in these systems.
Finally, studying the amount and evolution of the old non-thermal plasma in galaxy groups is important for understanding how diffuse radio sources, such as halos and relics, form in merging galaxy clusters (e.g. van Weeren et al., 2019). Observations indicate that the power of these radio sources strongly depends on the mass of the system (e.g. de Gasperin et al., 2014; Duchesne et al., 2021; Cuciti et al., 2021), consistent with the idea that a fraction of the total merger energy (up to ∼ 10 64 erg, Ferrari et al. 2008; Brunetti and Jones 2014) is converted into turbulence and shocks, which then generate the observed radio emission. Most halos and relics are indeed found in clusters more massive than 5 × 10 14 𝑀 ⊙ .
